-1 Introduction
-Worldwide, poor air quality is a leading preventable cause of death and morbidity (Fuller
-et al., 2022). Air pollution reduces the life expectancy of North India’s roughly half a billion
-residents by up to seven years, which represents one of the largest health burdens from
-pollution in the world (Lee and Greenstone, 2021). The use of fires to clear agricultural land
-in India, as in other low- and middle-income countries, is a major source of this pollution.
-Despite a clear economic case for reducing this pollution, as well as efforts to prohibit and
-fine those who produce it, agricultural pollution in North India has increased over the last
-few decades (Appendix Figure A.1). Arguably, existing policies have failed to account for
-incentives of two groups of actors: local officials’ incentives to enforce penalties when the
-costs and benefits of polluting activities are in different political jurisdictions, and farmers’
-incentives to protect the environment given that the costs of pollution are largely borne by
-others.1 In this paper, we ask whether a policy that explicitly considers these incentives can
-reduce pollution.
-Every winter, farmers in North India burn rice stalk (residue) to clear fields. Smoke
-blankets the region and spreads downwind to major population centers such as New Delhi,
-affecting the health of millions of people (Cusworth et al., 2018).2 According to the Global
-Burden of Disease, crop residue burning caused 66,000 premature deaths in India in 2015
-(GlobalBurdenofDiseaseWorkingGroup,2018). Abackoftheenvelopecalculationsuggests
-around 8.7 million acres of annual burning across North India.3 Using these estimates and
-focusingonthemortalitycostalone, valuedatastatisticallifevalueof$1million, theaverage
-annual external damages per acre are $7,600.4 In comparison, paddy production generates
-1Dipoppa and Gulzar (2022) provide evidence of this inter-jurisdictional externality, which undermines
-state and local actors’ incentives to enforce fines and bans. Farmers’ political clout and equity concerns
-around penalizing small farmers undermine enforcement even further.
-2Beyond India, pollution from crop residue burning contributes to the pollution burden in China (Chen
-et al., 2017), Southeast Asia (Oanh et al., 2018), and Africa (Cassou, 2018).
-3Our estimate is based on paddy burning estimates from Liu et al. (2020), who estimate a burn rate of
-50% for all paddy acreage in Punjab, Haryana and Uttar Pradesh.
-4Climate change, labor productivity, and morbidity costs aren’t included. Murali et al. (2010) estimates
-that residue burning in India emits around 4 million metric tons of carbon dioxide equivalents each year, or",[],False,2025-11-04T08:44:44.897670
